The test replaces `xcbq.xcffib.xproto.NameError` with Python’s `NameError`, then calls `Colormap.alloc_color` with `_BadHexColor()`, `(18, 52, 86)`, and `"steelblue"`. In `alloc_color`, the outer `try` catches `xcffib.xproto.NameError` from `AllocNamedColor(...).reply()`, so `NameError` is caught internally. Inside that handler, `hex(color)` is wrapped in a nested `try/except ValueError`; `_BadHexColor.__index__` raises `ValueError`, so `ValueError` is also caught internally. `"steelblue"` succeeds in the outer `try`, so no new caught exception kind appears after those.

## Answer
{"caught_exception_kinds":["NameError","ValueError"]}
